<section class="case-study__section reveal-on-scroll">
  <p>This project was different. No interviews scheduled in advance, no screener, no Zoom link. Just two days on the museum floor with a tally sheet, watching how 747 visitors discovered and used digital interactive screens across ten gallery locations. Where behaviour prompted a question, visitors were approached and asked about their experience on the spot, connecting the numbers to the why.</p>
  <p>The brief was to understand what was working, what wasn't and why, to inform future investment in the museum's digital offering.</p>
  <p>The clearest finding ran counter to the assumption the museum had been designing around: adults engage with interactive and game-like screens just as much as children do, often more. The catwalk simulator in Fashion and Style generated some of the longest dwell times in the dataset, almost entirely from adults. The framing of these screens as child-facing was actively excluding a significant portion of the real audience.</p>
  <p>The other central principle that emerged: digital screens earn their place when they do something the physical exhibit cannot. Video outperformed static content consistently across locations. Screens that duplicated what was already on the label were ignored.</p>
  <p>The research also surfaced a multilingual accessibility gap. A notable number of visitors approached for interviews couldn't participate due to limited English. Every screen in the museum was English only.</p>
</section>
